{
  "image_name": "1b9d7e9e424365077c441a238fad6327.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how DNA methylation dynamics help control normal cell differentiation, induced pluripotent stem cell reprogramming, and two broad cancer trajectories. At the top, mutant IDH is shown converting the normal metabolite pathway toward 2-hydroxyglutarate, which inhibits TET enzymes. TET promotes DNA demethylation, whereas DNMT3A promotes methylation, so the balance between these activities shapes cell-state gene programs.\n\nAcross the main panels, three cell states are compared—stem, proliferating, and differentiated—along with associated gene modules. In normal differentiation, the process is hierarchical: stem-cell-associated programs are progressively silenced and differentiation programs become established through increasing methylation at some loci, while TET-mediated demethylation acts at others. In contrast, iPSC generation is depicted as a reversible path back toward stemness, requiring erasure of differentiation-linked methylation patterns.\n\nThe cancer panels suggest two distinct epigenetic routes. In cancers with a stem cell origin, mutations such as DNMT3A loss or IDH/TET disruption block proper differentiation and maintain a stem-like hierarchy. In cancers arising from non-stem cells, abnormal TET-linked demethylation enables reversible phenotype switching, allowing differentiated or proliferating cells to reacquire stem-like features. Overall, the image emphasizes epigenetic plasticity as a central determinant of cell identity and tumor behavior.",
